#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fx-cloud: run heavy work in Vercel Sandboxes and keep everything else on this Mac.

Each agent gets its own sandbox for each folder it works in. The first command starts one from the
base snapshot; later commands reuse it. A sandbox ends when the agent runs `fx-cloud done`, when it
is idle for FXC_IDLE_MINUTES (30), or soon after the fx process that owns it ends. The owner is the
nearest fx process above the command (FXC_OWNER names another owner, for example a review run).

Run from the project folder on the Mac (fx uses these through its shell tool):
  fx-cloud run [--force] [--include PATH]... <command>
                                     copy this folder to the sandbox, then run the command there;
                                     output streams back and the exit code is the sandbox's
  fx-cloud run --no-push <command>   run without copying, for example to install a tool
  fx-cloud push [--force]            only copy this folder to the sandbox
  fx-cloud agent [--resume ID] [--test-lane] [--include PATH]... "<task>"
                                     hand a task to fx in the sandbox copy; prints its answer,
                                     its session ID, and the files it changed
  fx-cloud pull --patch [--apply]    the sandbox copy's changes as a patch; --apply applies it here
  fx-cloud pull <path> [<dest>]      copy a file or folder from the sandbox copy to the Mac
  fx-cloud macos-build [zig args]    build a Zig project's macOS binary in the sandbox and put it
                                     in this worktree's zig-out/bin, for hands-on checks on the Mac

The copy lives at the same path in the sandbox.
- A git worktree copy matches the Mac: the commit, uncommitted and new files. Ignored files stay on
  the Mac unless a command needs them: name them with --include, or list them in .fx-cloud-include.
  The Mac is the source of truth: a copy that the sandbox changed is never overwritten without
  --force; pull its changes first.
- A folder outside git gets a one-way copy, up to 200 MB, without build and dependency folders.
  Sandbox changes there can be overwritten, and pull --patch does not work.
A command that runs keeps its sandbox alive, however long it takes.

Sandboxes:
  fx-cloud done [--all]      remove this agent's sandbox for this folder (--all: for every folder)
  fx-cloud ls                list the agent sandboxes, their owners and folders
  fx-cloud gc [--all]        remove sandboxes whose agent or folder is gone (--all: every one)
  fx-cloud shell [--base]    open a shell in this agent's sandbox for this folder (or the base)
  fx-cloud status            show the base snapshot, the agent sandboxes, and both fx builds
  fx-cloud base [--no-warm]  rebuild the base snapshot: toolchain, fx login, test key, warm caches
                             (`setup` does the same)
  fx-cloud login             one-time sign-ins in the base: fx login and the aig test key

Settings come from FXC_TEAM, FXC_PROJECT, FXC_VCPUS (8), FXC_IDLE_MINUTES (30), FXC_MAX_AGENTS (24),
FXC_OWNER and FXC_REMOTE_WS. FXC_SANDBOX=<name> sends every command to that one sandbox instead.
"""
